# Exact cached post-live --all findings; answers explain only the anchors of their class.

[step] These exact picks lie on the raised identifiers or numeric/convention markings named in this part's manifest label windows. The font is Arial bold: 4.0 mm horizontal identifiers/conventions (4.3 mm for the basin title), or 3.6 mm tangent-aligned side angle values. Relief is nominally 0.48 mm, with the blade's side identifier projecting 0.49 mm. Their narrow readings include curved terminals and corners of letters, rather than nominal structural walls. The current emitted-width overlays in lettering-review.json identify these markings; physical legibility is unrecorded.
alternatives: Larger 4.8 mm glyphs or a stroke-expanded font can enlarge terminals, but need wider pads or revised side layouts to keep the full words and angle values on their backing. A separate larger label plate provides more room but adds another removable identity whose association with the sample must be maintained. Increasing relief enlarges the projecting side marks without enlarging their planar stroke widths.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/r45-support-ceiling.step
click: x=0.643 y=9.563 z=23.480

[sliver] This 1.00 mm wide top strip is the exposed rear margin of the 3.00 mm thick base outside the 4.00 mm upright, not a one-millimetre wall or free fin. The base spans 30 by 9 mm and carries the two underside storage sockets; the upright starts at Y 9 and ends at Y 13 while the base reaches Y 14. Preserve the same heel on all three ceilings.
alternatives: A deeper base adds a broader margin but changes the common footprint and storage clearance. Extending the upright to the rear base edge removes the strip by adding stock, but thickens the upright and changes this cantilever's backing stiffness. Cutting the strip off narrows the base without enlarging any structural section.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/r45-support-ceiling.step
click: x=0.000 y=13.500 z=3.000

[ceiling] This is the required identical 3.00 mm thick, 30 by 21 mm flat cantilever ceiling at Z 17.00. It deliberately receives automatic supports so R30/R45/R60 can compare top-distance settings with three open removal directions: front -Y and both X sides. Native first roof nozzle Z 17.24 with 0.24 mm model step gives underside Z 17.00; highest supports Z 16.76/16.52/16.28 command 0.24/0.48/0.72 mm air for requested 0.30/0.45/0.60 mm. Physical removal and sag of these new specimens are unrecorded.
alternatives: A self-supporting sloped roof or a bed-facing separately printed roof avoids this ceiling but removes the supported flat surface that the comparison measures. Increasing roof thickness improves stock while changing cooling and bending during removal. A shorter cantilever changes its span and support contact area rather than isolating support distance.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/r45-support-ceiling.step
click: x=0.000 y=-1.500 z=17.000

[ceiling] These are the blind underside storage sockets: diameter 6.30 mm, roof Z 2.25, mating the 6.00 mm carrier pegs of height 2.00 mm. The 0.15 mm radial air and 0.25 mm top air keep these gravity-parked samples removable. Their bed-facing openings provide removal access; the saved project retains its automatic support decisions. This describes nominal docking geometry, not measured release force or roof strength.
alternatives: An open through-bore eliminates the socket roof but cuts the sample's upper face or active comparison surface. A 3.00 mm roof requires 2.25 mm more local stock above the current 3.00 mm heel; it raises or enlarges the heel and needs new display placements. A shallower peg preserves a thicker roof but reduces the two-millimetre storage engagement.
